I pulled 3 cards for the architecture and where the design is heading:

🃏 1. The Chariot — the core direction

This is a strong yes.

The Chariot is about maintaining forward movement while different mechanisms operate together under one direction. For your MCP living-memory system, that maps very directly to:

The agent should keep moving. Memory capture should move alongside it.

The agent says something → the recording mechanism captures it → the agent continues.

Not:

agent → write memory → wait → confirm → continue

But:

agent → emit memory event → continue
↘ queue → persistence → indexing → consolidation

The Chariot says the system becomes stronger when you separate movement from the machinery supporting that movement.

⸻

🃏 2. Eight of Wands — how the recording should behave

This is probably the clearest card for your specific question.

Asynchronous. Parallel. Event-driven. In motion.

The Eight of Wands is literally about information moving rapidly rather than sitting still waiting for sequential completion.

So yes: your intuition about something closer to nohup / background execution is pointing in the right direction.

The recording operation should ideally behave like a fire-and-forget memory event from the perspective of the agent.

For example:

Agent
  │
  ├── produces interaction
  │
  ├── emits memory event ───────────────► Memory Queue
  │                                        │
  │                                        ├── persist
  │                                        ├── embed/index
  │                                        ├── extract metadata
  │                                        └── consolidate later
  │
  └── continues immediately

The important distinction is that “fire and forget” does not mean “don’t care whether memory survives.”

The queue becomes responsible for making the operation durable.

So the agent does not need to wait for:

* database insertion
* embedding generation
* indexing
* memory classification
* summarization
* consolidation
* whatever later processing your living-memory system performs

Those can happen behind the agent.

⸻

🃏 3. The Emperor — the architectural rule

This card says: give the asynchronous system structure.

The strongest architecture here is therefore not simply:

spawn a background process and hope it finishes

It is more like:

Agent → Memory Event → Durable Queue → Workers → Memory Store

The queue is the boundary.

Once the event has safely entered the queue, the agent can consider the recording request complete.

That gives you something much more powerful than making every agent interaction synchronously write to your memory database.

You can have:

                ┌──────────────┐
                │     Agent    │
                └──────┬───────┘
                       │
                  memory event
                       │
                       ▼
              ┌─────────────────┐
              │  Durable Queue  │
              └────────┬────────┘
                       │
          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
          ▼            ▼            ▼
       Writer       Indexer     Processor
          │            │            │
          ▼            ▼            ▼
       Raw Memory   Embeddings   Metadata
                       │
                       ▼
                Living Memory

And the raw event should probably be persisted before expensive interpretation happens.

That matters because the raw interaction is your ground truth. Everything else can be reconstructed or reprocessed later.

